Setting (notes c. 1978-81 on the 'Teichmüller tower', before the Esquisse). G is a stable 'MD graph' (probably Mumford–Deligne), i.e. the dual graph of a stable marked curve. It has edges A, legs I and Â = A ⊔ I. M_G (= RP_G) is the corresponding boundary stratum of M̄_{g,ν}, essentially the product of the M_{g_v,n_v} over the vertices.

For each a ∈ Â there is a G_m-torsor P_{G,a} over M_G:
- for an edge, the pair of tangent lines at the two branches of the node (the smoothing parameter, i.e. the normal direction to the boundary divisor);
- for a leg, the tangent line at the marked point minus zero.
For C ⊂ Â, set P_{G,C} = fibre product over M_G of the P_{G,a}, a ∈ C. This gives a G_m^C-torsor, with special cases
- SP_G = P_{G,Â} (full 'tangential base point' data at nodes and punctures);
- P_G = P_{G,A};
- SRP_G = P_{G,I};
- RP_G = P_{G,∅} = M_G.
The lower-right square records SP_G → P_G, SRP_G → RP_G as G_m^I-torsors and SP_G → SRP_G, P_G → RP_G as G_m^A-torsors, forming a cartesian square.

The page's main content concerns a 'generalization' G → G' ≅ G/C, the contraction of a set C ⊂ A of edges. Write A' = A∖C for the edges of G'; G^♮ is the fully contracted one-vertex graph, i.e. the open stratum M_{g,ν}. M_{G,G'} denotes the deleted tubular neighbourhood of M_G inside the stratum M_{G'}. Up to homotopy it is the G_m^C-torsor P_{G,C}; in particular P_G ≃ M_{G,G^♮}.

This yields a ladder of torsors. Its rows are
- SP_G → SP_{G'} → SP_{G^♮};
- P_G → P_{G'} → P_{G^♮} = M_{G^♮};
- P_{G,C} → RP_{G'} = M_{G'};
- then down to RP_G = M_G.
Its vertical maps are G_m^I-, G_m^{A'}- and G_m^C-torsors, and the squares are cartesian. Grothendieck stresses that the horizontal maps (deleted neighbourhood ↪ open stratum) exist only in the homotopy/topological world, not algebraically. They are equivariant for G_m^I, G_m^{A'} and G_m^{Â'} = G_m^I × G_m^{A'}.

Applying Π_1 gives the groupoid version. T_G = Π_1 P_G is an extension of RT_G = Π_1 M_G by Z^A (Dehn twists about the vanishing cycles). ST_G is a further Z^I-extension (twists about punctures, i.e. the pure ribbon / tangential-base-point version). T_{G,C} = Π_1 P_{G,C}. The diagram
ST_G → ST_{G'} → ST_{G^♮}; T_G → T_{G'} → T_{G^♮}; T_{G,C} → RT_{G'}; → RT_G
has cartesian squares of extensions by Z^I, Z^{A'} and Z^C.

Purpose: this is the functoriality of the 'groupoids at infinity' of the Teichmüller tower along the stratification. It lets the stable Teichmüller groupoids be assembled from the pieces T_{g,ν} and their central-type extensions. It continues the previous page's reduction to constructing the ST_{g,I} and their Z-extensions S(T_{g,I}),i.
